-- ==========================================================================
-- unit11-modules-projects.sokonanoda — Unit 11 · Modules & Projects
--
-- Rules:
--   * A declaration containing sorry is an exercise. Replace the sorry with
--     your answer; the full kernel decides right or wrong.
--   * Partial answers are a legal state: write the first few fun layers and
--     leave the last one as sorry.
--   * Judgment always goes through the kernel: every filled line stays valid
--     in official Lean.
--
-- The first ten units wrote proofs inside **one file**. A real project is "one
-- entry file plus its import closure": shared names live in a library module
-- and the entry file imports it on line one. This unit teaches that boundary —
-- the top-of-file syntax, module names ↔ paths, the project manifest and the
-- closure-level rules — ending with a real two-file project under
-- course/unit11-project/ (its cross-file exercises are in Exercises.sokonanoda).
-- This canvas itself is still a single file: a course unit is one file.
-- ==========================================================================

-- Skeleton (this file plays the part of the **imported library module**:
-- And axioms + the Or inductive + an Iff definition, all old friends from the
-- earlier units; once an entry imports it, these names are directly usable):
axiom And : Prop -> Prop -> Prop
axiom And.intro : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> a -> b -> And a b
axiom And.left : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> And a b -> a
axiom And.right : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> And a b -> b

inductive Or (A B : Prop) : Prop
ctor inl (a : A) : Or A B
ctor inr (b : B) : Or A B
end

def Iff (A : Prop) (B : Prop) : Prop := And (A -> B) (B -> A)

-- Demo (filled in): the library's first export — swap the two halves of an And.
-- Exercise 5 reuses it.
theorem and_comm (A : Prop) (B : Prop) (h : And A B) : And B A :=
  And.intro B A (And.right A B h) (And.left A B h)

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.1 Why import: drawing a boundary around names
--
-- The single-file world runs on an implicit convention: each file declares the
-- names it needs, so one name can mean different things — Unit 9's `Or` is a
-- real inductive while Unit 1's `Or` is only an axiom; paste both into one file
-- and they collide into "this name is already declared". import makes "which
-- Or?" answerable: names belong to **modules**, and the module boundary is the
-- boundary. Three rules to start with:
--   (1) every top-level name of an imported module is **globally visible** in
--       the whole closure (there is no export list);
--   (2) so a cross-module **clash is an error** (import-name-collision): the
--       fix is to declare the name once;
--   (3) "entry file + closure" is one **compilation unit**, and the kernel is
--       still the only judge.
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.2 `import Foo.Bar`: at the very top of the file
--
-- The shape matches official Lean: `import` + a dotted module name, one per
-- line, and it **must come before any declaration** (otherwise the error is
-- import-must-precede-declarations: move the line to the top of the file).
--
--   import Logic            -- the Logic.sokonanoda next to this file
--   import Lesson.Logic     -- Logic.sokonanoda in the Lesson/ subdirectory
--
-- Example: the first line of course/unit11-project/Canvas.sokonanoda is
-- `import Logic`, and right below it And and And.intro can be used because
-- those names are declared in Logic.sokonanoda. `import` is a command keyword:
-- the name position stays lax (`def import := 1` is legal) while the expression
-- position is rejected (`def x : Nat := import` reports "cannot appear inside
-- an expression").
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.3 Module names come from paths: the file name is the module name
--
-- `import Foo.Bar` resolves to `<module root>/Foo/Bar.sokonanoda`: every
-- component must be a legal identifier (starting with a letter or underscore),
-- only `.` separates them, and the `/` in the path is filled in by the
-- compiler; matching is case-sensitive. This rule decides which files **cannot
-- be imported**: `-` is not an identifier character, so
-- course/unit1-propositions-proofs.sokonanoda cannot be imported — writing
-- `import unit1-propositions-proofs` gives import-not-a-valid-module-name
-- (hint: a module name cannot contain `-`), while `import unit1` gives
-- import-not-found, whose message even asks "did you mean to import
-- unit1-propositions-proofs.sokonanoda?". To make a file importable, give it a
-- legal identifier name, such as `Logic.sokonanoda`.
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.4 Project root and module root: sokonanoda.toml
--
-- Walk **up** from the entry file's directory to the nearest
-- `sokonanoda.toml`: that is the project root, and the module root defaults to
-- the manifest's directory. The manifest **may be an empty file** (its
-- existence is the marker); three optional keys:
--
--   name = "unit11-project"   -- project name, metadata, not used by resolution
--   requires = "0.56"         -- pins the toolchain: a mismatch only warns
--   src = "src"               -- module root, default "." (relative to the manifest)
--
-- With no manifest at all there is a **zero-config fallback**: the module root
-- is the entry file's own directory, so "two files next to each other that
-- import each other" needs no manifest — single files are the default and
-- projects are opt-in.
-- Two CLI switches: `--root <dir>` sets the module root explicitly (skipping
-- discovery); `--no-project` ignores the manifest (module root = the entry
-- file's directory).
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.5 The whole closure is judged together
--
-- The compiler resolves the **closure** from the entry file and judges it in
-- topological order (dependencies first) inside a single environment, so all
-- four of these are **project-level** diagnostics:
--   * import-cycle             a cycle A → B → A: move the shared part into a
--                              lower-level module;
--   * import-name-collision    two modules declare the same top-level name:
--                              the message names both sources;
--   * import-dependency-failed a dependency failed to compile: only **one**
--                              report on the line that imports it — fix the
--                              dependency's first error instead of drowning in
--                              cascades;
--   * import-prelude-conflict  a prelude conflict (below).
-- prelude is a property of the **whole compilation unit**, decided by the entry
-- (a Bare entry makes the whole closure Bare); a dependency that writes **no**
-- directive inherits, and only an explicit directive that disagrees conflicts.
-- The other let-the-file-win rule is the **shared prelude module**: if any
-- module in the closure declares its own `inductive Nat` (like the hand-written
-- Nat blocks of Units 6/7), the whole closure skips the built-in Nat — a
-- library can define Nat once and share it.
-- The last one is the most common trap: when an imported module still has
-- `sorry`, the importer only gets a **warning** (import-has-open-exercises) —
-- holes never enter the environment, so those names are unusable while the rest
-- of that module's declarations work as usual.
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.6 Hands-on: run a real two-file project
--
--   course/unit11-project/
--   ├── sokonanoda.toml          -- manifest: name = "unit11-project"
--   ├── Logic.sokonanoda         -- library: axiom And / And.intro / And.left / And.right
--   ├── Canvas.sokonanoda        -- entry: `import Logic` + theorems that use it (filled in)
--   └── Exercises.sokonanoda     -- entry: `import Logic` + two exercises with sorry
--
-- From the repository root (judgment always goes through the kernel and needs
-- no Lean toolchain):
--   scripts/soko course/unit11-project/Canvas.sokonanoda         -- judge the closure
--   scripts/soko course/unit11-project/Exercises.sokonanoda      -- cross-file exercises
--   scripts/soko build course/unit11-project                     -- warm the cache
--
-- `build`'s cache key is the **hash of the whole closure**: edit
-- Logic.sokonanoda and the Canvas that imports it must recompile (a changed
-- dependency always misses the entry); leave the closure untouched and the
-- whole thing is a hit.
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.7 Exercise A: extend the library's exports
--
-- This file plays the shared library, so the exercises are "the export the
-- library owes". Each one may use only the names **already declared in this
-- file**: an exercise is an open declaration, its hole never enters the
-- environment, so do not reference another unsolved exercise.
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Exercise 1 (export X): the demo's sibling, but with the hypothesis written as
-- an arrow:
--
--   "And A B implies And B A."
--
-- soko:hint approach: the conclusion is `And A B -> And B A`, an arrow on the outside — start with fun (h : And A B) to bring the hypothesis in.
-- soko:hint goal shape: after the fun the goal is And B A; use And.intro B A to fill the two proof slots.
-- soko:hint key piece: both pieces of evidence come from h: And.right A B h proves B and And.left A B h proves A.
theorem and_comm_imp_x (A : Prop) (B : Prop) : And A B -> And B A :=
  sorry

-- Exercise 2 (export X): a library has `def`s too, not only theorems — pin the
-- left elimination of And as a library export (its type is A and its value is a
-- proof).
-- soko:hint approach: the declaration is def … (h : And A B) : A; h is already a parameter, so no fun is needed.
-- soko:hint goal shape: the goal is A and h : And A B is in hand.
-- soko:hint key piece: And.left takes three explicit arguments: A, B and h.
def and_left_x (A : Prop) (B : Prop) (h : And A B) : A :=
  sorry

-- Exercise 3 (export X): the library's inductive also needs exports: swap the
-- two halves of an Or.
-- soko:hint approach: h : Or A B carries the two branches, so split with match.
-- soko:hint goal shape: the goal is Or B A; the inl branch gets a : A and the inr branch gets b : B.
-- soko:hint key piece: the conclusion's parameters come in the order B then A: a goes to the "right" constructor (inr B A a) and b to the "left" one (inl B A b).
theorem or_comm_x (A : Prop) (B : Prop) : Or A B -> Or B A :=
  sorry

-- Exercise 4 (export X): this one is deliberately an anonymous `example` (the
-- form Unit 5 used): a library can also check a proposition without naming it.
--
--   "If A implies B and B implies A, then A and B are equivalent."
--
-- soko:hint approach: the two directions are two hypotheses (two arrows), and the conclusion packs that pair of functions into an Iff.
-- soko:hint goal shape: the conclusion has shape Iff A B, and Iff unfolds to And (A -> B) (B -> A).
-- soko:hint key piece: use And.intro with parameters (A -> B), (B -> A), putting mp and mpr in the two proof slots.
example (A : Prop) (B : Prop) (mp : A -> B) (mpr : B -> A) : Iff A B :=
  sorry

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.8 Exercise B: evaluate a module the closure rejected
--
-- This "second module" was rejected, and the error comes from the **whole
-- closure** rather than from the module itself:
--
--   -- Library2.sokonanoda
--   axiom And : Prop -> Prop -> Prop
--   theorem and_comm (A : Prop) (B : Prop) (h : And A B) : And B A :=
--     And.intro B A (And.right A B h) (And.left A B h)
--
-- The error is import-name-collision: `And` and `and_comm` were already
-- declared in the first module. Hint: the fix is not to dodge with a rename but
-- to **not declare it twice** — use the copy that import brings in.
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Exercise 5 (evaluate R): this file *is* that "first module": And and and_comm
-- are already declared here. Write one new export that hands the work to the
-- shared and_comm (that is the correct version of the module above).
-- soko:hint approach: do not copy the proof out; and_comm already exists in this file, so call it.
-- soko:hint goal shape: the goal is And B A; hand A, B and h to and_comm explicitly.
-- soko:hint key piece: the application is written `and_comm A B h` (fill in the same three arguments as in the demo, in order).
theorem and_comm_via_lib_x (A : Prop) (B : Prop) (h : And A B) : And B A :=
  sorry

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 11.9 Exercise C: synthesis
--
-- The last exercise wires the library's And elimination to Or construction and
-- uses let and match (old friends from Units 6/3). Work out "what does the goal
-- look like" first, then choose a rule for each step.
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Exercise 6 (synthesis ★★): one direction of distributivity — "A and (B or C)"
-- implies "(A and B) or (A and C)".
-- soko:hint approach: use let to name the right half of h (Or B C), then match on it; in both cases repack A with the matching half.
-- soko:hint goal shape: the outer layer is fun (h : And A (Or B C)); write let bc : Or B C := And.right A (Or B C) h; then match bc.
-- soko:hint key piece: take A from h with And.left and the branch evidence with And.right; the inl b branch packs And.intro A B and hands it to Or.inl, the inr c branch packs And.intro A C and hands it to Or.inr.
theorem and_or_imp_x (A : Prop) (B : Prop) (C : Prop) : And A (Or B C) -> Or (And A B) (And A C) :=
  sorry
